# Corner Grocer

## Link
A Link to the Corner Grocer [repository](https://github.com/kimarimt/CornerGrocer)

## Description
The program is designed to analyze text records generated by Corner Grocer throughout the day. These records contain a chronological list of items purchased during store operations. The program processes a specified record to determine the purchase frequency of each item and stores the resulting frequency data in a backup file. It also provides menu-driven interface that allows the user to search for a specific item and view its purchase count, display all items and their corresponding frequencies numerically, or visually as a histogram. 

## What Works Well
I did particularly well with defining the structure of the InventoryTracker class. Specifically, I clearly defined the program's state and associated functions while using appropriate access modifiers to support encapsulation. This helped keep the program organized by controlling access to class data and separating internal functionality from the methods that need to be accessible outside the class.

Additionally, I spent a lot of time making sure that the program was organized into clear and reusable functions. I separated key tasks such as processing the input file into an map data structure, saving the resulting map into a backup file. Specifically, I did well implementing the InventoryTracker::displayInventoryCount() method by including a Boolean parameter that toggles between displaying the counts numerically and visually. This approach allowed me to reuse the same method for two different menu options while avoiding unnecessary duplicate code.

## Improvements 
A key improvement I want to make to the code is separating the functions responsible for formatting the table headers, rows, and footer into their own class. This will help keep the InventoryTracker class and its associated methods more concise, focused specifically on managing inventory data and the menu-driven interface. Separating the formatting logic would also improve the organization and maintainability of the program by reducing the number of responsibilities handled by single class.

Another improvement I would make is adding support for processing multiple input files. Currently, the program is designed to process a single input file, which limits flexibility when working with purchase records from different days. Supporting multiple files would allow the program to combine and analyze inventory data across a larger period of time. This would make the program more scalable and useful with also reducing the need to manually combine records before processing them.

## Challenges
One of the most challenging parts of the project was implementing the file-processing logic. I had to review the course module that focused on file processing and conduct some additional research to fully understand how to use file streams properly. In particular, I needed to learn how to handle errors when opening a file and how to correctly read and process the file line by like. Working through these challenges helped strengthen my understanding of file input and error handling in C++

## Transferable Skills
One of the most transferable skills I gained from this project was learning how to organize a program using object-oriented programming principles. Designed the InventoryTracker class helped me better understand encapsulation, access modifiers, and separating a program into reusable methods with specific responsibilities. I can use these concepts in future project to write code that easier to understand, maintain, text, and expand. 

I also strengthened my skills in file processing, working with maps, and error handling. These skills are transferable to many software development projects because applications often need to read external data, organize and process that data efficiently, and handle unexpected errors without causing the program to fail.